#a�i	DEDR 301: 4. to be attached: n. love - DEDR 302: to become mellow (no noun)? The DED adds a�itu etc. under 301 which is possible but not totally convincing. The word minly seems to be used for lovelorn creatures which are to be pitied, but there are occasional overtones of a second level of meaning where the notion of love is triggered. Without detiled investigation all that can be sid is that the noun somehow seems to oscillate between pity and love. The derivations have to be analysed as appellative nouns (partly employed as denominatives), as they use no aspectual infixes. Only exception is the imperative a�imati, but here the question arises whether the verb is a secondary formation, since it clearly means "to pity" (not DEDR).

